    "id" : "python.requests.best-practice.use-raise-for-status.use-raise-for-status",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce use of raise_for_status() after HTTP requests",
    "description" : "Ensures that the raise_for_status() method is called after making HTTP requests with the requests library to catch HTTP errors.",
    "explanation" : "## use-raise-for-status\nThere's an HTTP request made with requests, but the raise_for_status() utility method isn't used. This can result in request errors going unnoticed and your code behaving in unexpected ways, such as if your authorization API returns a 500 error while you're only checking for a 401.",
