  {
    "id" : "scala_cookie_rule-TrustBoundaryViolation",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "InputValidation",
    "title" : "Avoid Trust Boundary Violation",
    "description" : "Detects when untrusted and trusted data are combined without proper validation, violating the trust boundary in a Scala program.",
    "explanation" : "## scala_cookie_rule-TrustBoundaryViolation\nA trust boundary can be thought of as line drawn through a program. On one side\nof the line, data is untrusted. On the other side of the line, data is assumed\nto be trustworthy. The purpose of validation logic is to allow data to safely\ncross the trust boundary - to move from untrusted to trusted. A trust boundary\nviolation occurs when a program blurs the line between what is trusted and what\nis untrusted. By combining trusted and untrusted data in the same data\nstructure, it becomes easier for programmers to mistakenly trust unvalidated\ndata.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Scala"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "scala_cors_rule-PermissiveCORS",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "HTTP",
    "title" : "Avoid Permissive CORS Policy",
    "description" : "Detects overly permissive Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) policies that allow any domain to access resources, which can lead to security vulnerabilities.",
    "explanation" : "## scala_cors_rule-PermissiveCORS\nPrior to HTML5, Web browsers enforced the Same Origin Policy which ensures that in order for\nJavaScript to access the contents of a Web page, both the JavaScript and the Web page must\noriginate from the same domain. Without the Same Origin Policy, a malicious website could serve\nup JavaScript that loads sensitive information from other websites using a client's\ncredentials, cull through it, and communicate it back to the attacker. HTML5 makes it possible\nfor JavaScript to access data across domains if a new HTTP header called\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin is defined. With this header, a Web server defines which other\ndomains are allowed to access its domain using cross-origin requests. However, caution should\nbe taken when defining the header because an overly permissive CORS policy will allow a\nmalicious application to communicate with the victim application in an inappropriate way,\nleading to spoofing, data theft, relay and other attacks.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Scala"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "scala_cors_rule-PermissiveCORSInjection",
    "level" : "Error",
    "category" : "Security",
    "subCategory" : "HTTP",
    "title" : "Avoid Permissive CORS Injection",
    "description" : "Detects overly permissive Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) policies that allow unrestricted access to resources from any origin.",
    "explanation" : "## scala_cors_rule-PermissiveCORSInjection\nPrior to HTML5, Web browsers enforced the Same Origin Policy which ensures that in order for\nJavaScript to access the contents of a Web page, both the JavaScript and the Web page must\noriginate from the same domain. Without the Same Origin Policy, a malicious website could serve\nup JavaScript that loads sensitive information from other websites using a client's\ncredentials, cull through it, and communicate it back to the attacker. HTML5 makes it possible\nfor JavaScript to access data across domains if a new HTTP header called\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin is defined. With this header, a Web server defines which other\ndomains are allowed to access its domain using cross-origin requests. However, caution should\nbe taken when defining the header because an overly permissive CORS policy will allow a\nmalicious application to communicate with the victim application in an inappropriate way,\nleading to spoofing, data theft, relay and other attacks.",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 15,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
      {
        "name" : "Java"
      }
    ]
